James inquired about the relationship between Dynamic Stochastic General Equilibrium (DSGE) and dynamic-recursive Computable General Equilibrium (CGE) models. I explained that both are used in economic modeling but have different purposes and assumptions. DSGE models focus on macroeconomic analysis, incorporating stochastic elements and optimizing behavior under rational expectations, while dynamic-recursive CGE models project the economy through a sequence of static equilibria without stochastic components, often used for policy analysis. Their relationship lies in their dynamic nature for assessing policy impacts, but they differ in complexity and assumptions. DSGE models emphasize theoretical consistency, while CGE models are more flexible and data-driven for policy simulations.
